The face diagonal is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle formed by the two face edges.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0,2.8);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,below] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,left] {$7\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}d^{2}&=7^{2}+10^{2}\\d^{2}&=149\\d&=\sqrt{149}\text{ cm}\\d&\approx12.21\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

A section through the cone's axis gives a right-angled triangle with the radius and perpendicular height as its shorter sides.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1.5,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0,3.5);
\draw (O)--(R)--(T)--cycle;
\draw (0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25);
\path (O)--(R) node[midway,below] {$3\text{ cm}$};
\path (O)--(T) node[midway,left] {$7\text{ cm}$};
\path (R)--(T) node[midway,above right] {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}s^{2}&=3^{2}+7^{2}\\s^{2}&=58\\s&=\sqrt{58}\\s&\approx7.6\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The longest rod joins opposite points on the bottom and top rims. A section through the cylinder's axis gives a rectangle; the rod is its diagonal.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2.4,3.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,3.4);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(C);
\draw (2.15,0)--(2.15,0.25)--(2.4,0.25);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,below] {$12\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$17\text{ cm}$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above left] {$L$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Use the diameter and height as the shorter sides.

$\begin{aligned}L^{2}&=12^{2}+17^{2}\\L^{2}&=433\\L&=\sqrt{433}\\L&\approx20.8\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The slant height is the hypotenuse. First find the radius $r$ by subtracting the squares.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2.94,0);
\coordinate (T) at (0,3);
\draw (O)--(R)--(T)--cycle;
\draw (0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25);
\path (O)--(R) node[midway,below] {$r$};
\path (O)--(T) node[midway,left] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\path (R)--(T) node[midway,above right] {$14\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}r^{2}+10^{2}&=14^{2}\\r^{2}&=196-100\\r&=\sqrt{96}\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The diameter is twice the radius. Keep the radius exact until the final calculation.

$\begin{aligned}d&=2\sqrt{96}\\d&\approx19.60\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

First convert the radius to the diameter: $d=2\times4=8\text{ cm}$.

A section through the axis gives a rectangle with the rod as its diagonal.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.45,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.45,1.6);
\coordinate (D) at (0,1.6);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(C);
\draw (3.2,0)--(3.2,0.25)--(3.45,0.25);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,below] {$l$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$8\text{ cm}$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above left] {$19\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The cylinder length is a shorter side, so subtract the squares.

$\begin{aligned}l^{2}+8^{2}&=19^{2}\\l^{2}&=361-64\\l&=\sqrt{297}\\l&\approx17.23\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

First find the base diagonal $AC$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.2,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (O) at (1.6,1.2);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (A)--(C);
\draw (2.95,0)--(2.95,0.25)--(3.2,0.25);
\fill (O) circle (1.2pt);
\path (A)--(B) node[midway,below] {$8\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$};
\path (C) node[above right] {$C$};
\path (O) node[above left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}AC^{2}&=8^{2}+6^{2}\\AC^{2}&=100\\AC&=10\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The centre bisects the diagonal, so $OC=\frac{10}{2}=5\text{ cm}$.

Since the apex is directly above $O$, triangle $EOC$ is right-angled at $O$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.5,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,3.6);
\draw (O)--(C)--(E)--cycle;
\draw (0.25,0)--(0.25,0.25)--(0,0.25);
\path (O)--(C) node[midway,below] {$5\text{ cm}$};
\path (O)--(E) node[midway,left] {$h$};
\path (C)--(E) node[midway,above right] {$13\text{ cm}$};
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\path (C) node[below right] {$C$};
\path (E) node[above] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}h^{2}+5^{2}&=13^{2}\\h^{2}&=169-25\\h&=\sqrt{144}\\h&=12.0\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$
